serial: in canonical mode, ^C (without ISIG) and ^D end a read

Purpose: a command that reads the console in canonical mode ("cat" with no
file, "cat /dev/ttyACM0", dd) could not be ended from the terminal: the
serial driver has no VEOF and, with ISIG off, ^C was an ordinary byte, so
the shell never came back without re-enumerating the USB device.  Now ^D
ends the read with what has been read (end-of-file at the start of a line,
POSIX VEOF) and ^C, when ISIG is off, ends it as end-of-file.  Nothing is
killed and no signal is sent.  readline() reads in non-canonical mode and
still gets both bytes (it handles ^C itself).
Zephyr: the Zephyr shell has no command that reads the console, so it
always comes back; a Ctrl-C typed while a command runs is handled at the
next prompt.  Builtin applications that do not read the console are not
affected: their Ctrl-C stays in the buffer for the next prompt.

diff --git a/drivers/serial/serial.c b/drivers/serial/serial.c
index 772ea5eb..6aa6360f 100644
--- a/drivers/serial/serial.c
+++ b/drivers/serial/serial.c
@@ -1119,6 +1119,26 @@ static ssize_t uart_readv(FAR struct file *filep, FAR struct uio *uio)
                 }
             }
 
+          /* In canonical mode, ^D (VEOF) ends the read with what has been
+           * read so far (end-of-file when nothing has), and ^C without
+           * ISIG ends it as end-of-file, dropping a partial line.  So a
+           * command that reads the console (e.g. "cat" with no file) can
+           * always be ended from the terminal; readline() reads in
+           * non-canonical mode and sees both bytes as input.
+           */
+
+          if ((dev->tc_lflag & ICANON) != 0 &&
+              (ch == ASCII_EOT ||
+               (ch == ASCII_ETX && (dev->tc_lflag & ISIG) == 0)))
+            {
+              if (ch == ASCII_ETX)
+                {
+                  recvd = 0;
+                }
+
+              break;
+            }
+
           if ((dev->tc_lflag & ICANON) &&
               (ch == ASCII_BS || ch == ASCII_DEL))
             {
